    1	# P1 — D-finite (Apéry-style) recurrence hunt for δ(n)
    2	
    3	**Script:** `session63fresh_dfinite_delta.py`
    4	
    5	## What was tested
    6	
    7	A sequence (a_n) is D-finite (holonomic) if there exist polynomials
    8	P_0, ..., P_L ∈ ℤ[n], not all zero, with
    9	sum_{j=0..L} P_j(n) · a_{n+j} = 0. Apéry's recurrence for ζ(3) is
    10	L=2, deg=2.
    11	
    12	D-finite is *strictly larger* than the algebraic relations PSLQ-style
    13	searches probe — so this is a genuinely new test. If δ(n) = p(n) − R⁻¹(n)
    14	were D-finite of small order/degree, we would get a fast holonomic
    15	extrapolation algorithm, evaluating δ(n) in O((L+d)·M(log n)) and hence
    16	p(n) in polylog.
    17	
    18	## Method
    19	
    20	1. Compute δ(n) for n = 1..400 at 200-bit precision (mpmath Newton-on-R⁻¹).
    21	2. For each (L, d) ∈ {1..4} × {0..4}: build matrix A whose row at n has
    22	   entries n^k · δ(n+j) for (j, k) ∈ [0..L] × [0..d].
    23	3. Train on first half (n = 30..200), validate on second half (n = 201..395).
    24	4. Take smallest right-singular vector of column-normalized A_train as
    25	   candidate recurrence coefficients.
    26	5. **Critical step:** validate by *predicting* δ(n+L) from δ(n)..δ(n+L−1)
    27	   using the recurrence and comparing to the actual value. Skill =
    28	   RMSE(predicted − actual) / std(δ). Skill = 1.0 means no signal;
    29	   skill < 0.05 would mean a real recurrence.
    30	
